By definition, $\binom{n}{k} = \frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}.\text{ Therefore,}\end{think}
\[\frac{1}{\binom{n}{k}} = \frac{k!(n-k)!}{n!} \ge 0\]for every integer $k,$ with equality occurring if and only if $k=0$ or $k=n.$ Hence,
\[\sum_{k = 0}^n \frac{1}{\binom{n}{k}} \le \sum_{k = 0}^n \binom{n}{k} = \boxed{2^n}.\end{think}
\begin{align*}b_n &= \sum_{k = 0}^n \frac{k}{\binom{n}{k}}\\ &= \sum_{k = 0}^n \frac{k \cdot k! (n-k)!}{n!}\\ &= \sum_{k = 1}^{n - 1} \frac{k \cdot k! (n-k)!}{n!}\end{align*}
If $0 \le k \le n,$ then $k \le n$ and $k < n$ imply that $\frac{k}{n} < 1.$  Thus,
\[\frac{k \cdot k! (n-k)!}{n!} < \frac{(k + 1) \cdot (k + 1)! (n - (k + 1))!}{n!}\]as long as $0 \le k \le n.$ Conversely, $$\frac{(k + 1) \cdot (k + 1)! (n - (k + 1))!}{n!} < \frac{k \cdot k! (n-k)!}{n!}.$$Because the terms in the product do not change sign, the summation with the $<$ and the one with the $>$ hold simultaneously, so that $$\frac{k \cdot k! (n-k)!}{n!} < \frac{(k + 1) \cdot (k + 1)! (n - (k + 1))!}{n!}$$for $0 \le k \le n$ with $k$ an integer.  Therefore, $$\sum_{k = 0}^n \frac{k}{\binom{n}{k}} < \sum_{k = 0}^n \frac{(k + 1)}{\binom{n}{k + 1}}.$$By definition, $\binom{n}{k + 1} = \frac{n!}{(k+1)!(n-k-1)!}.$  As such, $$\sum_{k = 0}^n \frac{(k + 1)}{\binom{n}{k + 1}} = \sum_{k = 0}^n \frac{n!}{\binom{n}{k + 1} (k + 1)} = \sum_{k = 0}^n \frac{n!}{n!} = \boxed{n + 1}.$$</think> <answer>$\frac{2^n}{n + 1}$</answer>

